* O(1) - We only need a constant amount of space to store the indices of the two numbers.\\n\",\n179:         \"- **Why this approach:** This approach is straightforward and easy to understand but is not efficient for large lists due to its quadratic time complexity.\\n\\n\",\n180:         \n181:         \"```python\\n\",\n182:         \"def twoSum_bruteForce(nums, target):\\n\",\n183:         \"    for i in range(len(nums)):\\n\",\n184:         \"        for j in range(i + 1, len(nums)):\\n\",\n185:         \"            if nums[i] + nums[j] == target:\\n\",\n186:         \"                return [i, j]\\n\",\n187:         \"```\\n\\n\",\n188:         \n189:         \"### ⚡ Approach 2: Hash Table Method\\n\",\n190:         \"- **Algorithm:** We can use a hash table (dictionary in Python) to store the numbers we've seen so far and their indices. \",\n191:         \"For each number, we check if its complement (target - number) is in the hash table.\\n\",\n192:         \"- **Time Complexity:** O(n) - We make a single pass through the list of numbers.\\n\",\n193:         \"- **Space Complexity:** O(n) - In the worst case, we might store every number in the hash table.\\n\",\n194:         \"- **Why this is better:** This approach is much more efficient than the brute force method, especially for large lists, because it reduces the time complexity to linear.\\n\\n\",\n195:         \n196:         \"```python\\n\",\n197:         \"def twoSum_hashTable(nums, target):\\n\",\n198:         \"    num_dict = {}\\n\",\n199:         \"    for i, num in enumerate(nums):\\n\",\n200:         \"        complement = target - num\\n\",\n201:         \"        if complement in num_dict:\\n\",\n202:         \"            return [num_dict[complement], i]\\n\",\n203:         \"        num_dict[num] = i\\n\",\n204:         \"```\\n\\n\",\n205:         \n206:         \"## 🔍 Implementation Details\\n\",\n207:         \"- **Edge Cases:** The hash table approach handles edge cases well, such as when the list is empty or contains only one element, \",\n208:         \"because it simply returns without finding a solution in such cases.\\n\",\n209:         \"- **Testing Strategy:** Key test cases include lists with two numbers that sum to the target, lists with no such pair, \",\n210:         \"and edge cases like empty lists or lists with a single element.\\n\",\n211:         \"- **Trade-offs:** The brute force method is simpler but much less efficient for large inputs, \",\n212:         \"while the hash table method is more efficient but uses more memory.\\n\",\n213:         \"- **Real-world Context:** The two-sum problem is a basic example of how hash tables can be used to solve problems efficiently, \",\n214:         \"which is a common pattern in many real-world applications, such as data processing and algorithmic challenges.\"\n215:     ];\n216:     \n217:     // Get the streaming markdown parser from the live interview UI\n218:     const parser = window.liveInterviewUI?.markdownParser;\n219:     if (!parser) {\n220:         console.error('❌ Markdown parser not found. Make sure live interview UI is initialized.');\n221:         return;\n222:     }\n223:     \n224:     // Reset parser for test\n225:     parser.reset();\n226:     \n227:     // Create a test output container\n228:     let testContainer = document.getElementById('sample-markdown-test');\n229:     if (!testContainer) {\n230:         testContainer = document.createElement('div');\n231:         testContainer.id = 'sample-markdown-test';\n232:         testContainer.style.cssText = `\n233:             position: fixed;\n234:             top: 10px;\n235:             left: 10px;\n236:             width: 50%;\n237:             max-height: 80vh;\n238:             background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.95);\n239:             border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.2);\n240:             border-radius: 8px;\n241:             padding: 16px;\n242:             color: white;\n243:             font-family: inherit;\n244:             font-size: 14px;\n245:             overflow-y: auto;\n246:             z-index: 10000;\n247:             backdrop-filter: blur(10px);\n248:         `;\n249:         \n250:         // Add close button\n251:         const closeBtn = document.createElement('button');\n252:         closeBtn.textContent = '×';\n253:         closeBtn.style.cssText = `\n254:             position: absolute;\n255:             top: 8px;\n256:             right: 8px;\n257:             background: none;\n258:             border: none;\n259:             color: white;\n260:             font-size: 18px;\n261:             cursor: pointer;\n262:             width: 24px;\n263:             height: 24px;\n264:             display: flex;\n265:             align-items: center;\n266:             justify-content: center;\n267:         `;\n268:         closeBtn.onclick = () => testContainer.remove();\n269:         testContainer.appendChild(closeBtn);\n270:         \n271:         // Add title\n272:         const title = document.createElement('h3');\n273:         title.textContent = '🧪 Sample Markdown Real-Time Test';\n274:         title.style.cssText = 'margin: 0 0 12px 0; color: #64d2ff;';\n275:         testContainer.appendChild(title);\n276:         \n277:         // Add content area\n278:         const content = document.createElement('div');\n279:         content.id = 'sample-test-content';\n280:         content.style.cssText = 'line-height: 1.5;';\n281:         testContainer.appendChild(content);\n282:         \n283:         document.body.appendChild(testContainer);\n284:     }\n285:     \n286:     const contentArea = testContainer.querySelector('#sample-test-content');\n287:     \n288:     // Function to simulate streaming\n289:     let chunkIndex = 0;\n290:     const streamChunk = () => {\n291:         if (chunkIndex < sampleChunks.length) {\n292:             const chunk = sampleChunks[chunkIndex];\n293:             console.log(`📝 Processing sample chunk ${chunkIndex + 1}/${sampleChunks.length}:`, JSON.stringify(chunk.substring(0, 50) + '...'));\n294:             \n295:             // Process through parser\n296:             const renderedHTML = parser.processChunk(chunk);\n297:             \n298:             // Update display\n299:             contentArea.innerHTML = renderedHTML;\n300:             \n301:             chunkIndex++;\n302:             \n303:             // Continue streaming with realistic delay\n304:             setTimeout(streamChunk, 200 + Math.random() * 300);\n305:         } else {\n306:             // Finalize\n307:             console.log('✅ Sample streaming complete, finalizing...');\n308:             const finalHTML = parser.finalize();\n309:             contentArea.innerHTML = finalHTML;\n310:             console.log('🎉 Sample markdown real-time test completed!');\n311:             \n312:             // Start auto-close countdown\n313:             startAutoCloseCountdown(testContainer, 10);\n314:         }\n315:     };\n316:     \n317:     // Start the test\n318:     console.log('🚀 Starting sample markdown streaming test...');\n319:     setTimeout(streamChunk, 500);\n320: }\n321: \n322: /**\n323:  * Auto-close countdown for test windows\n324:  */\n325: function startAutoCloseCountdown(container, seconds) {\n326:     let remainingSeconds = seconds;\n327:     \n328:     // Create countdown display\n329:     const countdownDiv = document.createElement('div');\n330:     countdownDiv.style.cssText = `\n331:         position: absolute;\n332:         bottom: 8px;\n333:         right: 8px;\n334:         background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.8);\n335:         color: #fbbf24;\n336:         padding: 4px 8px;\n337:         border-radius: 12px;\n338:         font-size: 11px;\n339:         font-weight: 500;\n340:         border: 1px solid rgba(251, 191, 36, 0.3);\n341:         backdrop-filter: blur(5px);\n342:         z-index: 10001;\n343:     `;\n344:     container.appendChild(countdownDiv);\n345:     \n346:     // Update countdown every second